QueryClient, Provider and
Important Defaults
Install v5.103.1 and build a predictable cache boundary

Phase 22 - TanStack Query
Volume 06 - Professional Frontend

Install the two Phase 22 packages
cd apps/web
pnpm add @tanstack/react-query@5.103.1
pnpm add -D @tanstack/react-query-devtools@5.103.1

Create one stable browser QueryClient
The provider is a Client Component because QueryClientProvider uses React context. The rest of the
App Router tree does not become client-only merely because it is passed through this provider.

// apps/web/src/lib/query/get-query-client.ts
import { environmentManager, QueryClient } from "@tanstack/react-query";

function makeQueryClient() {
return new QueryClient({
defaultOptions: {
queries: {
staleTime: 30_000,
retry: 2,
},
mutations: { retry: 0 },
},
});
}

let browserQueryClient: QueryClient | undefined;

export function getQueryClient() {
if (environmentManager.isServer()) return makeQueryClient();
if (!browserQueryClient) browserQueryClient = makeQueryClient();
return browserQueryClient;
}

// apps/web/src/app/providers.tsx
"use client";

import { QueryClientProvider } from "@tanstack/react-query";
import { ReactQueryDevtools } from "@tanstack/react-query-devtools";
import { getQueryClient } from "@/lib/query/get-query-client";

export function Providers({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
const queryClient = getQueryClient();
return (
<QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>
{children}
<ReactQueryDevtools initialIsOpen={false} />
</QueryClientProvider>
);
}

Why staleTime is not cache lifetime

staleTime controls when data is considered fresh. gcTime controls how long inactive query data
remains before garbage collection. They answer different questions. TaskFlow starts with a modest
30-second freshness window, then overrides it where the domain requires different behavior.

Current v5 default to remember

Cached query data is stale by default unless staleTime is configured. Inactive queries are garbage-collected
after five minutes by default. Client queries retry failures three times by default, but TaskFlow deliberately
starts with a smaller retry budget.

A default is a policy decision
Do not copy staleTime values from another application. A stock-price dashboard, an HR directory and a
static country list have different freshness expectations. Global defaults are baselines; individual
queries can override them when the domain requires it.

Setting                      TaskFlow starting policy              Reason

staleTime                    30 seconds                            Avoid immediate duplicate refetch after
hydration while keeping Tasks reasonably fresh

gcTime                       library default                       No evidence yet that TaskFlow needs a different
inactive-cache lifetime

query retry                  up to 2 selected transient failures   Avoid long retry loops while tolerating brief
transport faults

mutation retry               0                                     Writes should not repeat automatically unless
idempotency is designed

